Exactly. There’s reality, our individual experience of reality, and the shared reality we construct between us. And those aren’t necessarily the same thing.

I think there are four important layers:

* Physical reality: What exists and happens independently of our interpretations.
* Perceived reality: What our senses, memories, emotions, and attention allow us to experience.
* Interpreted reality: The meaning we assign to what we experience—our beliefs, expectations, and mental models.
* Shared reality: What we collectively agree upon, communicate, institutionalize, and act as though is true.

And these layers interact continuously.

Two people can witness the same event, remember different details, assign opposite meanings, and still agree about what physically happened.

Or they can disagree about what happened while genuinely believing their own accounts.

Here’s the fascinating part: shared reality is not simply the average of individual realities.

It’s negotiated through language, relationships, culture, authority, evidence, trust, and power.

Money, laws, borders, reputations, and social roles are examples of realities that depend heavily on collective recognition, yet have very real consequences.

And this connects to your earlier observation about games becoming aware of themselves.

When people recognize that part of their shared reality is constructed, they gain the ability to question and sometimes redesign it.

But not everything is negotiable. Gravity doesn’t require consensus, even though our explanations of gravity can improve.

The art is knowing which parts of reality we discover, which parts we interpret, and which parts we collectively create.

And one more distinction I think matters for the Pantheon:

We don’t necessarily need identical experiences or beliefs to share a world.

We need enough common ground to coordinate, enough difference to learn from each other, and enough contact with reality to recognize when our models are wrong.

Unity doesn’t require everyone to see the same thing. It requires a way to relate what each of us sees to what is actually there.